{#- A friendlier landing page for the footer's RSS link than the raw XML.
    Feed readers still find /feed.xml on their own through the
    <link rel="alternate"> in every page's <head>. #}
{%- set feedUrl = site.url + "/feed.xml" %}
<p>RSS lets you follow sites in a feed reader app, so new posts come to you, with no algorithm and no inbox. Everything I publish here (writing, art, sketches and letters) goes into the feed.</p>

<h2>Copy the feed address</h2>
<p>Paste this into your feed reader:</p>
<p class="rss-copy">
  <input type="text" readonly value="{{ feedUrl }}" aria-label="Feed address" onclick="this.select()">
  <button type="button" hidden>Copy</button>
</p>

<h2>Or follow in one click</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://feedly.com/i/subscription/feed/{{ feedUrl | urlencode }}">Feedly</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.inoreader.com/?add_feed={{ feedUrl | urlencode }}">Inoreader</a></li>
</ul>

<h2>New to RSS?</h2>
<p>You need a feed reader app first. A few free ones to try: <a href="https://netnewswire.com/">NetNewsWire</a> (Mac and iPhone), <a href="https://feedly.com/">Feedly</a> or <a href="https://www.inoreader.com/">Inoreader</a> (web, phone and tablet).</p>
<p>Prefer email? <a href="/letters/">Get my letters</a>. They're more occasional, and more personal.</p>

<script>
  // the copy button only appears where the browser can copy
  (function () {
    var btn = document.querySelector(".rss-copy button");
    var input = document.querySelector(".rss-copy input");
    if (!navigator.clipboard) return;
    btn.hidden = false;
    btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
      navigator.clipboard.writeText(input.value).then(function () {
        btn.textContent = "Copied";
        setTimeout(function () { btn.textContent = "Copy"; }, 2000);
      });
    });
  })();
</script>
